# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# EXP-1: the fog covers the squares the party has not been near, and
# nothing else on the screen.
#
# **A pair leg, and it can be one.** This seam claims no key — it is a
# setting, not a command (`seam_explored.cpp`) — so every keystroke in
# the script below reaches the program in both halves and the two runs
# differ by the flag alone. That is the one thing the journal's legs
# could not have (#234).
#
# **What changed with M5-E5f (#263).** The marking used to be a lift:
# the game's whole window as the game drew it, with the squares the party
# had walked one shade brighter. So the two runs were identical until the
# party took its first step, and the first `allow` below covered the
# whole arrival with `none`. The fog is the other way round — a map
# nobody has walked is a window that is nearly all fog — so the two runs
# now diverge at frame 10,275, which is where the arrival settles, and
# what this leg confines is the fog rather than the trail.
#
# **What the fog is, since this leg is what confines it.** A covered
# square is *half* covered: a one-pixel checkerboard of palette index 0,
# black, with the program's own pixel on the other half (M5-E5f #263 for
# the checker, after the maintainer chose it off five coverings
# composited over a real frame; M5-E5g #299 for the colour, after walking
# it). Neither colour moved a rect — confinement is about the window, and
# a checker reaches exactly the squares a solid fill reached.
#
# **What M5-E5g (#299) did move is the rects**, because it moved the
# reveal radius from one to **zero**: the party sees the square it is
# standing on and no other, so the trail is one square wide instead of a
# three-wide corridor and everything either side of it is fog.
#
# **The rects are the fogged squares and not a box round them.** On this
# walk the clear squares are the middle column's own — the square the
# party is on and the ones it has stepped off — so the two columns each
# side of it are fog for their whole height, the middle column is fog
# ahead of the party, and behind the party it is fog until the party has
# actually stood there. Naming the window as one rectangle would have
# passed whatever the seam drew inside it; these five say where the
# boundary is.
#
# **The numbers below this line are the radius-one build's** (#263): 523
# dumped frames, 112 of them differing from the seam-off run, every
# difference inside that build's four rects and not one pixel outside the
# window at any frame. This leg has **not been driven since #299** — no
# visual leg has, because #291 made every one of them a script that no
# longer boots the way it was recorded, and #293 is the pass that owes
# them all a re-drive. The rects and the phase boundaries below are
# derived from the geometry and the key cadence, and confirming them on a
# real run is part of that pass.
about the fog covers the squares the party has not been near, and nothing else
kind pair
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
# The code-wheel bypass is gated on the wheel itself (#115), and both
# sides of this pair drive past the challenge, so both need it. Pinned
# by digest because a document never enters this tree.
document 0db301aeac4d2ec1e63b409ca6d3c9d39c63381d6712ff2ce7edc0528c6586fd
seam explored
until 260000000
every 25
# A screen may appear in the two runs a dump apart, because a repaint
# lands where it lands. One dump of slack, the same as every other leg.
slack 1

# Slot J: the one shipped save whose party is already standing on a
# wilderness area (`docs/explored-overlay.md` §4). Eight steps north,
# 150 frames apart, which is the cadence every driven walk in this tree
# uses.
key A@7601
key Return@7651
key L@8951
key J@9201
key Up@10600
key Up@10750
key Up@10900
key Up@11050
key Up@11200
key Up@11350
key Up@11500
key Up@11650

# The window is 120 by 120 at (8, 8) and a square is 24 by 24, so these
# are whole squares of it. The party is the window's middle column
# throughout — it walks due north and the window scrolls with it — so:
# the two columns west of it and the two east of it are fog for their
# whole height; `ahead` is its own column above it; `behind` is its own
# column below it, which is fog until it has stood there; and `tail` is
# the lower half of `behind`, which stays fog for one step longer.
rect ahead 56,8,79,55
rect west 8,8,55,127
rect east 80,8,127,127
rect behind 56,80,79,127
rect tail 56,104,79,127

# The whole run up to the arrival on the wilderness map: not one pixel.
# The mode byte becomes 3 at frame 9,552 and the screen settles at
# 10,275, which is where the fog first appears.
allow 0-10250 none
# Arrived, with one square stood on: everything but that square.
allow 10275-10650 ahead west east behind
# One step north (the key at 10,600, allowed the same 75 frames to land
# as every other phase boundary here): the square the party came from is
# window row 3 and is clear, and row 4 is still fog.
allow 10675-10800 ahead west east tail
# From the second step on, the two squares below the party are both
# squares it has stood on, and the trail is one column wide.
allow 10825-end ahead west east
